<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pierre-Joseph Proudhon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1809–1865</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pierre-Joseph Proudhon was a French socialist thinker and the first person to call himself an anarchist. His book What Is Property? of 1840 answered that 'property is theft', and he advocated mutualism, a society of independent producers exchanging through free credit and voluntary contracts without a centralised state. His followers were influential in the French labour movement, the First International and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Commune.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-joseph-proudhon.jpg" alt="Pierre-Joseph Proudhon" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustave Courbet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Pierre_Joseph_Proudhon_1865.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
